CAT = "iespHealth"; SEG = "HLT"
# Units 11 and 12 of Safe Pass: personal health and welfare, noise and vibration. General Application
# Regulations 2007 Part 5 (noise: lower action 80 dB(A), upper action 85 dB(A), limit 87 dB(A);
# hand-arm vibration action 2.5 m/s2, limit 5 m/s2, A(8)); HSA noise FAQ, HAV risk assessment and
# sun protection pages; Asbestos regulations as summarised by the HSA.
Q = [
("A worker's daily noise exposure reaches 82 dB(A). What must the employer do as a minimum?",
 'Make hearing protection available', ['Nothing until 90 dB(A)', 'Stop the work immediately', 'Move the worker to nights'],
 '80 dB(A) is the lower exposure action value in the General Application Regulations 2007, at which hearing protection must be made available. At 85 dB(A) it must be worn.'),
('Above which daily noise exposure must hearing protection zones be marked and protection worn?',
 '85 dB(A)', ['75 dB(A)', '80 dB(A)', '90 dB(A)'],
 'At the upper exposure action value of 85 dB(A) the employer must make sure hearing protection is used, and the areas are marked as hearing protection zones.'),
('What daily noise exposure must never be exceeded at the ear, even allowing for hearing protection?',
 '87 dB(A)', ['80 dB(A)', '85 dB(A)', '100 dB(A)'],
 '87 dB(A) is the exposure limit value in the Irish noise regulations, assessed taking account of the hearing protection worn.'),
('What rough test suggests noise in an area is above the action level?',
 'Shouting is needed to talk at 2 metres', ['A radio can be heard from indoors', 'A phone ringing goes unnoticed once', 'The noise lasts for over an hour'],
 'The HSA says if it is difficult to hear normal conversation at 2 metres, the noise is likely to be above 80 dB(A).'),
('What is the main long-term effect of repeated loud noise at work?',
 'Permanent hearing loss', ['Short-sightedness', 'Dermatitis on the hands', 'Weakened bones in old age'],
 'Noise-induced hearing loss builds up over years and cannot be cured. Tinnitus, a ringing in the ears, is another lasting effect.'),
('Which of these tools is most likely to cause hand-arm vibration syndrome?',
 'A breaker or an angle grinder', ['A spirit level or square', 'A tape measure or chalk line', 'A paint brush or roller'],
 'Hand-held powered tools such as breakers, grinders and hammer drills pass vibration into the hands and arms.'),
('What is vibration white finger?',
 'Finger circulation damaged by vibration', ['A skin rash caused by wet cement', 'Frostbite from working in very cold weather', 'A fungal infection of the nails'],
 'Vibration white finger is part of hand-arm vibration syndrome. Fingers go white and numb, especially in the cold, and the damage can be permanent.'),
('Which early sign of hand-arm vibration syndrome should a worker report?',
 'Tingling or numbness in the fingers', ['Sore feet at the end of a shift', 'A stiff neck first thing in the morning', 'Sunburn on the backs of the arms'],
 'The HSA says workers should be trained to recognise and report numbness and tingling early, before the condition worsens.'),
('What is the daily exposure action value for hand-arm vibration?',
 '2.5 m/s² A(8)', ['0.5 m/s² A(8)', '1.15 m/s² A(8)', '5 m/s² A(8)'],
 'The General Application Regulations 2007 set the hand-arm action value at 2.5 m/s² and the limit at 5 m/s². 0.5 and 1.15 are the whole-body values.'),
('Which step reduces hand-arm vibration exposure most?',
 'A different method or a low-vibration tool', ['Wearing anti-vibration gloves', 'Gripping the tool much harder', 'Working faster to finish sooner'],
 'The HSA says to avoid the process or use lower-vibration tools and less trigger time. It does not count anti-vibration gloves as a control, though gloves keep hands warm.'),
('Why does keeping hands warm help a worker who uses vibrating tools?',
 'It keeps blood flowing to the fingers', ["It lowers the tool's own vibration", 'It makes the tool feel lighter', 'It stops dust reaching the skin'],
 'Cold narrows the blood vessels and makes vibration damage worse. Warm gloves and warm breaks help circulation.'),
('Which illness can workers catch from rat urine on sites and in water?',
 "Leptospirosis (Weil's disease)", ["Legionnaires' disease", 'Tetanus from rusty nails', 'Hepatitis A from food'],
 'Leptospirosis enters through cuts or the eyes and mouth. Cover cuts, wash hands before eating and keep welfare areas clean.'),
('What should a worker do on finding material they suspect is asbestos?',
 'Stop work and report it', ['Break a piece off to check it', 'Sweep it up and bin it', 'Wet it and keep cutting'],
 'The HSA says that if you uncover hidden material or dust you suspect may contain asbestos, stop work and get competent advice. Disturbing it releases fibres that can cause cancer.'),
('How should silica dust from cutting concrete be controlled?',
 'Water suppression or on-tool extraction', ['Cutting faster to finish sooner', 'Opening the site gates for air', 'Brushing the dust away afterwards'],
 'Respirable crystalline silica causes lung disease. Wet cutting or on-tool extraction stops dust at source; RPE is added where needed.'),
('Why should workers outdoors protect their skin from the sun?',
 'Skin cancer is common in outdoor workers', ['Sunlight makes their PPE fade', 'Sunlight weakens the scaffold tubes', 'Sunlight drains tool batteries'],
 'The HSA says skin cancer is the most common cancer in Ireland and more common in outdoor workers. Cover up, use sunscreen and take shade.'),
('What is the most common cause of manual handling injuries?',
 'Lifting with a bent back or twisting', ['Lifting with the knees bent', 'Keeping the load close to the body as you lift', 'Planning the route before lifting'],
 'Bending and twisting while lifting strains the back. Keep the load close, bend the knees, avoid twisting and get help or a lifting aid.'),
('What should an employer do first about heavy manual handling?',
 'Avoid it with mechanical aids', ['Train everyone to lift more weight', 'Hand out back support belts', 'Rotate the strongest workers'],
 'The General Application Regulations require employers to avoid manual handling where possible, for example with hoists, trolleys and smaller loads.'),
('Which welfare facility must a construction site provide?',
 'Toilets and washing facilities', ['A gym for the workforce', 'Free car parking for all', 'A covered smoking shelter'],
 'The Construction Regulations 2013 require sanitary conveniences, washing facilities with hot and cold or warm running water, potable drinking water, a sheltered place to take meals and a way of drying wet clothing.'),
('A plasterer goes straight from mixing to eating a sandwich in the van. What is the health risk?',
 'Swallowing harmful substances', ['Catching a cold from the van', 'Damaging their hearing', 'Straining their back'],
 'Cement, lead, oils and bacteria on the hands can be swallowed with food. Washing hands before eating removes them.'),
('What skin problem can wet cement cause?',
 'Dermatitis and burns', ['Vibration white finger', 'Noise-induced hearing loss', 'Sunstroke and heat stress'],
 'Wet cement is alkaline and can cause dermatitis and chemical burns. Wear gloves and long sleeves and wash off any cement promptly.'),
]
